  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAn expansive area of high pressure was centered over\nsouthwestern Quebec and stretched west and southwestward into \nthe Upper Midwest and Plains. This was leading to mainly clear \nskies across the Northland. However, southerly upslope flow \nacross the Northern Plains was leading to an expansive area of \ncloud cover with some shower activity across North Dakota in \nassociation with a mid-level shortwave. Heading into tonight, \nthe surface high pressure will move a bit more to the east as an\nupper ridge moves overhead. This will bring that southerly flow\nacross the Northland as a warm front approaches from the west. \nShowers will begin to spread in late tonight across north- \ncentral Minnesota and spread across much of northeastern \nMinnesota through Thursday morning. This activity will weaken as\nit moves east as it runs into the strong high pressure to our \neast and dry air in the low levels. Models have continued their \nupward trend in rainfall amounts across north-central Minnesota \nwith a quarter to a half inch being possible along with isolated\nhigher amounts to near three-quarters of an inch from the \nBrainerd area northward into western Koochiching County. \nRainfall amounts fall off quickly to the east with little to no \nrain expected across northwest Wisconsin and along the immediate\nNorth Shore. Highs tomorrow will be in the upper 50s to upper \n60s with the warmest temperatures across northwest Wisconsin. \n\nFriday looks mainly dry with surface high pressure and upper\nridging in place. Southerly flow will also persist and bring\nwarmer air into the region giving highs in the 60s and lower\n70s, coolest along the North Shore. The upper ridge axis will\nshift to our east on Saturday as another shortwave lifts\nnortheastward along the periphery of the ridge across the\nNorthern Plains. This will bring another chance for showers and\nperhaps a few rumbles of thunder to mainly northeastern\nMinnesota Saturday into Saturday night. Guidance has trended\ndownward with QPF amounts over the last few runs with this\nsystem. Highs this weekend will remain in the 60s with a few low\n70s. \n\nThe pattern looks to remain active into next week with multiple\nchances for rainfall. A stronger area of low pressure looks to\nmove from the Plains through the Great Lakes during this time\nperiod. While the operational GFS and ECMWF show this system,\nthere are timing and magnitude differences between them with the\nGFS being quicker and more compact. In either case, widespread\nbeneficial rainfall looks possible. Temperatures will remain\nabove normal to start the week before trending cooler with \nupper 50s and lower 60s by Wednesday. These values would\nactually be near to slightly below normal for late September.",
